WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.960
Will Captain Pike's first officer be sent&nbsp;
to prison? Have we really seen the last of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.520
Chief Engineer Hemmer? And when will James&nbsp;
T. Kirk show up again? These are just some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:14.160
of the questions left unanswered at the end&nbsp;
of Season 1 of Star Trek: Strange New Worlds.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.680
Captain Pike's tough-as-nails first officer&nbsp;
Number One holds near-legendary status in Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.440
Trek history. Previously only seen in the original&nbsp;
series episode "The Menagerie," which used clips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.920
from the unaired original pilot episode, fans&nbsp;
have always been fascinated by the character.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.760
Strange New Worlds finally reveals&nbsp;
Number One's real name, Una Chin-Riley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.000
and gives us her backstory as a&nbsp;
genetically modified Illyrian.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.760
In the episode Ghosts of Illyria it's&nbsp;
said that because of the Federation's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.160
ban on genetic tampering, people like Number&nbsp;
One are barred from serving in Starfleet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.320
As such, her Illyrian heritage has&nbsp;
been kept a closely guarded secret.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.320
When Captain Pike learns her true nature,&nbsp;
he vows to never reveal it because she is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.480
the finest first officer in the fleet.&nbsp;
Yet, somehow, the information gets out.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.120
In the season finale, Pike's colleague&nbsp;
and lover Captain Batel beams aboard the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.240
Enterprise with a security detail and arrests&nbsp;
Number One for the crime of being an Illyrian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:13.120
We're curious about what will happen now, and&nbsp;
who leaked the information to Starfleet. The fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.800
that franchise canon has never mentioned her could&nbsp;
suggest she spends the rest of her days in prison.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.569
Either way, it would seem that Number One's fate&nbsp;
will be a major story heading into Season 2.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.960
Hard-nosed Enterprise security chief La'An&nbsp;
Noonien-Singh's last name hints at a frightening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.760
lineage. In Ghosts of Illyria, we learn that&nbsp;
one of her ancestors is none other than Ricardo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:40.240
Montalbán's villainous Khan. But it was La'An's&nbsp;
tragic backstory that added pathos to the series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.320
In Memento Mori we learn La'An&nbsp;
was rescued as a child by Number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.600
One from a Gorn breeding planet&nbsp;
after the Gorn killed her family.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.120
La'An uses her experience with the Gorn to&nbsp;
defeat a swarm of Gorn ships in the episode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.960
Then in All Those Who Wander she gets a&nbsp;
measure of revenge by killing three Gorn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.120
hatchlings and rescuing a young girl.&nbsp;
Perhaps reminded of her own ordeal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.760
La'An takes a leave of absence to help the&nbsp;
child locate her family. But will she return?

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:11.280
We know actor Christina Chong will be back for&nbsp;
Season 2. But will we follow her quest to reunite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.760
the girl with her family or see her return to a&nbsp;
different Starfleet ship? Chong has revealed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.240
Cinema Blend that she has filmed scenes with&nbsp;
James T. Kirk actor Paul Wesley, stating:

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.840
"I can't speak on story, but I can&nbsp;
say that Paul Wesley is very funny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.280
and I love working with him."

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.920
The alternate future Pike glimpsed in the season&nbsp;
finale does show La'An serving as a commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.280
aboard Kirk's ship. This may suggest La'An&nbsp;
eventually finds a home on the U.S.S. Farragut.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.520
The cast of Star Trek: Strange New Worlds&nbsp;
Season 1 combined old favorites like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:44.320
Captain Pike and Mr. Spock with exciting new&nbsp;
characters like chief engineer Lt. Hemmer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.720
The series' most distinct alien officer&nbsp;
and biggest breakout character, Hemmer is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.400
an Aenar — an offshoot of the Andorians — who&nbsp;
possesses a form of precognition and telepathy.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.920
By the end of the first season, however,&nbsp;
Hemmer sacrifices himself to save the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.080
rest of his shipmates. His death leaves&nbsp;
the ship without a chief engineer. Is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.760
possible that Chief Engineer Montgomery&nbsp;
Scott will make his debut next season?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.760
Or are we due for another new character&nbsp;
to work miracles in the engine room?

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.215
"I like this ship! You know, it's exciting!"

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:19.040
Actor Bruce Horak, who plays Hemmer, has said he's&nbsp;
not done with the series in several interviews.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.800
Whether this means Hemmer's telepathy&nbsp;
allowed his consciousness to survive like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.080
Spock's in Star Trek III or that&nbsp;
he will somehow be resurrected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.480
perhaps the next chief&nbsp;
engineer will still be Hemmer.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.960
Though fans were excited to see Pike,&nbsp;
Spock, and Number One in Strange New Worlds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:38.480
anticipation reached another level when Paramount&nbsp;
announced that James T. Kirk would be in Season 2.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.800
According to canon, Pike and Kirk met&nbsp;
only briefly. The Strange New Worlds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.040
Season 1 finale snuck actor Paul Wesley&nbsp;
into the role for a "what if" story that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.720
took place in the future, but Kirk's&nbsp;
role for Season 2 remains a mystery.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.320
We know Kirk is currently a young&nbsp;
lieutenant serving aboard the U.S.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.240
Farragut. One possibility is that&nbsp;
Season 2 may involve dual stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.240
set aboard the Enterprise and Farragut,&nbsp;
with Kirk receiving a recurring role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.240
Based on the original Star Trek we know&nbsp;
Kirk and Spock knew each other for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.880
so it's likely Spock will get some serious&nbsp;
screen time with his future Enterprise captain.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:14.720
However it shakes out, Wesley impressed&nbsp;
as Kirk in his appearance in A Quality of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.560
Mercy. Unless Paramount's announcement&nbsp;
was a ruse to hide the finale's plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.640
we should get plenty more of him next season.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.800
Since Strange New Worlds is set&nbsp;
nearly a decade before Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.000
The Original Series, it was&nbsp;
definitely unexpected to see actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Celia Rose Gooding playing Cadet Nyota&nbsp;
Uhura on the bridge of the Enterprise.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.400
Throughout Season 1, Uhura evolved&nbsp;
from a fresh-faced cadet to a strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.480
capable officer-in-training, growing close to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.160
chief engineer Hemmer while rotating&nbsp;
through every division on the ship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.560
But Uhura's dilemma persisted — does&nbsp;
she really want to serve in Starfleet?

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.800
Uhura makes it clear she may not want to&nbsp;
remain on the Enterprise, unsure if this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.640
is the right path for her. But thanks to&nbsp;
her friendship with engineer Hemmer — and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.200
ultimately, his sacrifice — it seems&nbsp;
Uhura may have finally decided to stay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.840
However, will she be awarded a full rank of&nbsp;
ensign, an "acting ensign" provisional title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.920
or could she return to Starfleet Academy?&nbsp;
We do know she'll be back on the Enterprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:12.668
by the time Kirk takes command, but what&nbsp;
happens in the meantime is anyone's guess.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:18.080
Sonequa Martin-Green's Michael Burnham wasn't&nbsp;
the first lost Spock sibling to be retconned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.760
into the franchise. Way back in 1989, the fifth&nbsp;
Star Trek feature film introduced us to Sybok,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:29.360
the illegitimate son of Spock's father Sarek. He's&nbsp;
a Vulcan who rejected logic and became a fanatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:33.280
devoted to finding God at the center&nbsp;
of the galaxy. The film was met with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:38.280
plenty of jeers and no subsequent Star Trek&nbsp;
series or film dared to mention Sybok again.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:42.240
"No! No! It's not possible!"

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:47.200
All that changed in Strange New Worlds when the&nbsp;
episode The Serene Squall revealed Sybok is being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.840
held in the Vulcan rehabilitation facility&nbsp;
where Spock's betrothed T'Pring serves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.320
Spock notes that he has been told to avoid&nbsp;
contact with his half-brother at all costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:00.640
and the ominous reveal seems to imply&nbsp;
that the villainous Vulcan may yet return.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:04.880
It has even been rumored among fans that Sybok&nbsp;
could become the behind-the-scenes villain of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:10.240
Season 2. The fact that the episode never shows&nbsp;
his face seems like a strange creative choice if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:15.680
he wasn't set to return in some fashion. This way,&nbsp;
any actor could step into the role, and with Sam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.840
Witwer campaigning for the part on Twitter,&nbsp;
another appearance could be on the horizon.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:24.640
Pulled from a pair of appearances on the original&nbsp;
Star Trek series, Strange New Worlds made minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:29.280
character Dr. M'Benga a series regular,&nbsp;
and the Enterprise's chief medical officer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.200
Expanding on the little-explored role, we&nbsp;
learn his first name is Joseph and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.240
he has a daughter named Rukiya who is suffering&nbsp;
from a terminal disease. M'Benga's relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:42.640
with his daughter provides some of the most&nbsp;
touching and heartfelt moments in Season 1.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:48.240
Strangely, in his two appearances from the 1960s,&nbsp;
Dr. M'Benga actually serves under Dr. McCoy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:51.280
who is in charge of sickbay when&nbsp;
Kirk commands the Enterprise.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.520
What happens to M'Benga to bump him from the&nbsp;
commanding doctor aboard the flagship of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:00.560
Federation to a supporting surgeon? We know&nbsp;
M'Benga risked the lives of the Enterprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:05.280
crew in Ghosts of Illyria by not revealing that&nbsp;
his daughter was being held in the transporter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:08.320
Could this have been a violation&nbsp;
of regulations that gets M'Benga&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:12.000
demoted? Or is there another incident&nbsp;
lined up for a future episode that sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.160
M'Benga either forcibly removed from&nbsp;
his role or voluntarily stepping down?

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:19.440
With Season 2 already filmed, we may&nbsp;
not have to wait long to find out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.969
though there's nothing to say that the&nbsp;
change in rank takes place anytime soon.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:29.120
In the 1990s, Star Trek was one of the first&nbsp;
franchises that had multiple projects side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:34.240
by side, with both movies and TV shows running&nbsp;
together. Still, while a few characters moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:40.320
back and forth, few proper crossovers actually&nbsp;
materialized. With Marvel, DC, and others pushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:45.520
interconnected franchises, could Strange New&nbsp;
Worlds finally see a major Star Trek crossover?

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:51.120
When asked about future crossovers, franchise&nbsp;
overlord Alex Kurtzman told Deadline Podcast:

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:57.127
"I think crossovers are can be really, really&nbsp;
exciting. But they have to exist for a reason."

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:01.520
Strange New Worlds seems perfectly set&nbsp;
up for such an event, as it's set on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:06.240
franchise's most beloved starship and has&nbsp;
a cast of fan-favorite classic characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:10.640
What could be more exciting than Jean-Luc Picard&nbsp;
traveling back to the days of Captain Pike or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:15.760
Ethan Peck's Mr. Spock traveling to a parallel&nbsp;
universe and meeting Zachary Quinto's Spock?

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:20.240
No matter what form a crossover might take,&nbsp;
there's no doubt it would be a major event that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:25.360
would pull in massive viewership figures.&nbsp;
And with Paramount and CBS now officially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.760
merged after years of separation, there's&nbsp;
no longer any legal reason it can't be done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:33.687
Will that happen during Strange&nbsp;
New Worlds Season 2? We hope so.

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.560
Star Trek: The Original Series will be just&nbsp;
six years away when Season 2 of Strange New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:43.200
Worlds begins, so the series may begin to&nbsp;
include more characters we've seen before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.840
It already features Spock, Uhura, Nurse&nbsp;
Chapel, and M'Benga, but there's still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:51.840
room for plenty of old favorites. James&nbsp;
T. Kirk is set to play a regular role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:56.080
the second season but we don't know when&nbsp;
he'll take over from Christopher Pike.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:01.440
Likewise, Hikaru Sulu, Pavel Chekov, and&nbsp;
even Yeoman Rand could have predated Kirk's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.600
time in the captain's chair. Scotty could&nbsp;
be next in line to take over engineering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:08.880
after the death of Lt. Hemmer, but&nbsp;
is it also possible that a younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:13.280
Dr. McCoy could join the sickbay staff&nbsp;
on his way to taking over for M'Benga?

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:18.320
It's not just the main cast members either. The&nbsp;
Star Trek franchise is full of recurring roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:22.880
side characters, and prominent one-offs that&nbsp;
fans would love to see again. Gary Mitchell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:28.594
Elizabeth Dehner, Carol Marcus, and Matt Decker&nbsp;
could all make appearances on Strange New Worlds.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:32.160
The most common adversary on the&nbsp;
original Star Trek and beyond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:36.320
the ruthless, war-loving Klingons&nbsp;
have long plagued the Federation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.840
But in the first season of Strange New&nbsp;
Worlds, the Klingons barely get a mention.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:43.360
Re-introduced in Star Trek: Discovery&nbsp;
with a controversial new look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:47.920
it's possible that the producers simply wanted&nbsp;
to give the Klingons a breather. Or maybe they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:52.640
needed some time to figure out what to do with&nbsp;
them after the divisive reaction among fans.

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:57.760
By the time Season 2 of Strange New Worlds rolls&nbsp;
around, it will have been four years since we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:01.840
seen a Klingon in live action, and it may be&nbsp;
time to bring them out for a new adventure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:05.920
On the Star Trek after-show The Ready&nbsp;
Room it's been seriously implied that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:09.840
they could indeed be coming back for&nbsp;
the second season of Strange New Worlds.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.920
Babs Olusanmokun who plays Dr. M'Benga&nbsp;
mentioned his character has a bitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:17.680
relationship with the honor-bound alien&nbsp;
race that will be explored next year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:20.000
so perhaps we may see them right out of the gate.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:25.120
But the question remains, which Klingons we will&nbsp;
see? The ridged-forehead Klingons of Discovery?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:28.880
Or the bronze-toned, smooth-headed&nbsp;
Klingons of The Original Series?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:33.200
If we had to wager money, we might even&nbsp;
bet on seeing a new version of Kang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:38.418
Kor, or Koloth, those three Klingon warriors&nbsp;
who menaced Kirk in The Original Series.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:44.240
Spock's betrothed T'Pring got a sizable recurring&nbsp;
role in Season 1 of Strange New Worlds. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:49.200
original Star Trek episode Amok Time we learn&nbsp;
it was T'Pring's resentment of Spock's career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:52.560
in Starfleet that was partially to&nbsp;
blame for ending their betrothal.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:55.520
We also learn that while Spock&nbsp;
was serving on the Enterprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.520 --> 00:11:00.400
T'Pring was involved with a Vulcan named Stonn,&nbsp;
who she wished to marry. In Strange New Worlds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:04.240
we learn Stonn is a colleague of&nbsp;
T'Pring's at the Ankeshtan K'til Retreat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:08.240
a Vulcan criminal rehab colony on&nbsp;
the third moon of Omicron Lyrae.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.280
Stonn only gets a single line&nbsp;
of dialogue in this appearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:15.440
but It's still a major reveal that could&nbsp;
lead to a bigger role for Stonn in Season 2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.360
Is it possible we could see the beginnings of&nbsp;
his relationship with T'Pring which will cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:24.480
her to reconsider her commitment to Spock? With&nbsp;
Sybok possibly being set up as a major villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:29.532
a trip back to Ankeshtan K'til and the&nbsp;
return of Stonn seems like it's in the cards.

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:32.560
If you're a longtime fan&nbsp;
of the Star Trek franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:35.200
you may have noticed a pattern&nbsp;
in the first season of Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:39.120
Strange New Worlds where the series did&nbsp;
their own versions of classic episodes.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:42.640
There was the iconic "Prime Directive"&nbsp;
episode where the crew must decide whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:47.760
to reveal themselves to a pre-warp society.&nbsp;
We also saw a "disease on the ship" episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:51.360
a "fantasy come to life" episode,&nbsp;
and a "monster on the ship" episode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:55.840
The season finale even did a time travel&nbsp;
story in classic Star Trek fashion.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:59.760
But there are still plenty of classic&nbsp;
stories Strange New Worlds hasn't done yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:04.560
Perhaps in Season 2 we'll see a "crew member&nbsp;
on trial" episode. In fact, with Number One in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:09.200
custody of Starfleet Security at the close of&nbsp;
the cliffhanger finale, that one seems likely.

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:13.920
We might also see the age-old "omnipotent&nbsp;
being" episode, with the Enterprise encountering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:19.360
an all-powerful alien life form like Q,&nbsp;
Trelane, or the Organians that toys with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:24.800
the crew. While these tropes and time-tested&nbsp;
stories may be cliched and a little formulaic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:28.560
Strange New Worlds has found refreshing&nbsp;
ways of putting a modern spin on them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:32.679
We're looking forward to seeing what&nbsp;
classic tropes they could trot out next.

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:37.360
Episode 8 of Strange New Worlds Season&nbsp;
1 gave us perhaps our most puzzling and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:42.560
unexpected Easter egg. In "The Elysian Kingdom" we&nbsp;
finally get a good look at the storybook that Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:46.960
M'Benga has been reading to his daughter, and&nbsp;
the author is none other than Benny Russell.

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:51.200
First appearing in a vision the Bajoran&nbsp;
Prophets gave Captain Benjamin Sisko&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:54.240
in the acclaimed Deep Space Nine&nbsp;
episode Far Beyond the Stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:59.760
Russell was a fantasy author from the 1950s who&nbsp;
wrote stories set in the Star Trek universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:04.160
There has always been a lingering suggestion&nbsp;
that perhaps there was more to Sisko's vision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:08.960
than being simply a dream, but most fans had&nbsp;
brushed it aside as a mere fanciful thought.

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:13.360
Now, with Strange New Worlds declaring that Benny&nbsp;
Russell was a real author in-universe, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:18.640
more questions. Was this just a throwaway gag&nbsp;
to amuse producers and set-dressers? Or does it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:24.465
suggest that Sisko, who lived Russell's life for a&nbsp;
brief period, experienced something more profound?

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:29.154
"What if all of this is the illusion?"

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:31.964
We're starting to wonder the same thing ourselves.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:35.120
By returning to an ensemble&nbsp;
format, Strange New Worlds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:39.280
satisfied our wish to see the franchise&nbsp;
give the spotlight to its wider cast.

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:43.840
The first season spent time exploring Captain&nbsp;
Pike, Number One, and Mr. Spock's story arcs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:48.080
But there were episodes centering on&nbsp;
security chief La'An and Dr. M'Benga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:53.120
while major B-stories were given to Nurse Chapel&nbsp;
and Chief Engineer Hemmer. In fact, we arguably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:57.600
learn more about Uhura in the episode Children&nbsp;
of the Comet than we did in all of Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:03.440
The Original Series. Sadly, one major cast member&nbsp;
was absent from the party: Lt. Erica Ortegas.

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:07.680
Captain Pike's operations officer, Ortegas&nbsp;
is a navigator and weapons officer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:12.880
and she was always active in any scene where the&nbsp;
ship saw action. Actor Melissa Navia got some fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:17.520
scenes when off-duty and offered a few cracking&nbsp;
one-liners, but, unfortunately, she didn't get an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:22.400
episode devoted to her. Could Season 2 see the&nbsp;
series finally give the spotlight to Ortegas?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:27.529
Fans who love her go-get-em attitude are&nbsp;
certainly hoping that's the case, and so are we.

